# Security-scan suppressions — file-specific and narrow (gitignore-style).
#
# logs/subagent_stop.json is an untracked, gitignored hook artifact: a verbatim
# dump of a sub-agent's final message. The pattern-scanner "weak-hash-sha1"
# finding at this path is a hash-shaped string inside a logged
# "last_assistant_message" field — transcript CONTENT, not cryptographic usage
# in code. Not shipped, not application security surface.
logs/subagent_stop.json
#
# .mypy_cache/** is mypy's auto-generated, gitignored (.gitignore:58),
# untracked type-analysis cache (needs the trailing ** — this scanner's
# _glob_match requires an explicit ** to recurse into a directory; a bare
# ".mypy_cache/" only matches that exact literal path and silently ignores
# nothing). The pattern-scanner "unsafe-deserialization" findings at
# .mypy_cache/**/pickle.data.json and .../cPickle.data.json are the literal
# module names "pickle"/"cPickle" appearing as cached type-stub metadata — no deserialization happens here; these files are regenerated by mypy and never shipped, so this is not source code or application security surface.
.mypy_cache/**
